# Self-improving loop trigger

When Wyatt's message begins with `loop:`, treat the remaining text as the immutable task brief.
The graph owns plan evidence. The host owns Luna invocation and records its returned JSON through
the graph CLI. No stage may edit source, commit, push, deploy, or claim a player playtest PASS.

1. Read `AGENTS.md`, `docs/BRIEFING.md`, and the top of `docs/STATUS.md`. Bind the one active
   valid card ID. If no card is active, or the brief names a different card, ask Wyatt to select or
   activate the correct card. Do not replace shared `prompt.md` or `log.md`.
2. Start the graph with the complete task brief on standard input:
   `python .agent/bin/dev_graph.py start --card-id <active-card-id>`.
   Read the returned `run_id`. A dirty worktree, existing graph lock, or invalid start is a hard
   stop. Do not clean, stash, or retry.
3. Run the fixed maker bridge once:
   `python .agent/bin/dev_graph.py run-maker --run-id <run-id>`.
   Continue only when the returned state is `await_review` and contains matching `brief`,
   `baseline`, `plan`, `maker`, and `review_request` artifacts. A blocked state is terminal.
4. Spawn exactly one sequential Codex Luna sub-agent: model `gpt-5.6-luna`, reasoning `high`.
   Give it the graph run directory's `brief.md`, `plan.md`, `maker-receipt.json`, and
   `review-request.json`, plus relevant source. It is read-only: it writes no files and returns
   one JSON object only, with no Markdown or prose.
5. Luna's JSON must contain exactly `schema_version`, `card_id`, `graph_run_id`, `base_head`,
   `baseline_sha256`, `brief_sha256`, `maker_receipt_sha256`, `maker_run_id`,
   `maker_result_sha256`, `plan_sha256`, `review_request_id`, `reviewer_id`,
   `reasoning_effort`, `reviewed_at`, `verdict`, and `findings`. Set `reviewed_at` to a UTC
   timestamp inside the request's `created_at`–`deadline_at` window. Set
   `reviewer_id="gpt-5.6-luna"`, `reasoning_effort="high"`, and use `APPROVE`, `REJECT`, or
   `ESCALATE`. `REJECT` and `ESCALATE` require non-empty actionable findings. The host passes the
   unmodified JSON to `python .agent/bin/dev_graph.py submit-review --run-id <run-id>` on standard
   input. The graph, not Luna, writes `review.json`.
6. If Luna is unavailable, times out, uses a forbidden write, or returns invalid JSON, run
   `python .agent/bin/dev_graph.py fail-review --run-id <run-id> --reason <short-safe-reason>` and
   stop. Do not retry Luna, reuse another run's review, or create a replacement review by hand.
7. If the graph returns `await_ack`, show Wyatt the plan and review. Stop for the exact human
   acknowledgement `ack <card-id>`, then record it with
   `python .agent/bin/dev_graph.py ack --run-id <run-id> --text "ack <card-id>"`. The complete
   graph is terminal. Do not start Sol or an implementation maker run. Any later source apply is a
   separate normal repository action under the existing per-wave plan and ACK rules.

`ship it` is separate deployment authorization. Never print or request the API key in chat.
